# An SBOM for the shipped binary, generated and independently checked (2026-10-05)

`scripts/gen-sbom.py` writes a CycloneDX 1.6 JSON bill of materials for the artifact this
repository ships: the `chaos` binary built from `xai-grok-pager-bin`. It reuses the dependency
reading that the notices batch had to get right -- `scripts/notices_lib.py`, non-dev edges
only, over the same 1139 third-party packages -- so the two artifacts cannot drift into
describing two different builds. `scripts/ci/check-sbom.py` is a separate program that
re-derives the same facts from `cargo metadata` and refuses a document that misstates any of
them; it never imports the generator.

Headline figures for this workspace: 1227 components, 5130 dependency edges, 16 marked `scope:
optional`, two purl namespaces (1130 `pkg:cargo`, 97 `pkg:generic`), a `SHA-256` digest on
each of the 1130 crates.io components, 1 835 616 bytes, and two runs of one tree
byte-identical. One real defect was found by looking at that output rather than by a test: the
graph came out empty, and 51 fixtures were green while it was. Section 2 replays the defect,
section 3 shows the guard refusing it, section 8 follows the digests out to the bytes on disk
they name, and sections 9 and 10 are two mutation matrices -- 24 planted weakenings of the
guard and 19 of the generator, 43 caught, 0 survived, every source file restored and verified
with `cmp`.

== 0. What the generator reports about this workspace

The command that has to be run first, on the tree as committed:

    $ python3 scripts/gen-sbom.py --output /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-a.json
    gen-sbom: wrote /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-a.json; components: 1227 (third-party 1134, vendored 5, workspace 88); dependency edges: 5130; SHA-256 on 1130; serial urn:uuid:1fc225f9-2cf6-5b21-89a7-231d3c4d50f4

The same command again, and a byte comparison:

    $ python3 scripts/gen-sbom.py --output /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-b.json
    gen-sbom: wrote /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-b.json; components: 1227 (third-party 1134, vendored 5, workspace 88); dependency edges: 5130; SHA-256 on 1130; serial urn:uuid:1fc225f9-2cf6-5b21-89a7-231d3c4d50f4
    $ cmp /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-a.json /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-b.json
    exit: 0
    $ wc -c < /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-a.json
    1835616

1 835 616 bytes, same serial, no difference at any byte offset. The guard, reading the file as
a stranger would:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-sbom.py --sbom /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-a.json
    check-sbom: OK (1227 component(s): third-party 1139 (matching THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES), workspace 88; 5130 dependency edges; SHA-256 on 1130; serial urn:uuid:1fc225f9-2cf6-5b21-89a7-231d3c4d50f4)

Note the two third-party counts. The generator reports 1134 registry packages plus 5 vendored
crates under `third_party/`; the guard reports their union, 1139, because that is the number
the notices document has to cover and `check-notices-coverage.py` asserts 1139 on the same
tree. Both numbers are correct for their own question, which is why both are printed.

`--check` exists so that a committed or staged file can be tested for staleness without
rewriting it:

    $ python3 scripts/gen-sbom.py --check /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-a.json
    gen-sbom: /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-a.json is current; components: 1227 (third-party 1134, vendored 5, workspace 88); dependency edges: 5130; SHA-256 on 1130; serial urn:uuid:1fc225f9-2cf6-5b21-89a7-231d3c4d50f4
    exit: 0

== 1. What is inside the document, counted

The document is not a flat list, so the counts that matter are per-kind:

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    import json, collections
    d = json.load(open("/tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-a.json"))
    comps = d["components"]
    print("components", len(comps))
    print("purl namespaces", dict(collections.Counter(c["purl"].split("/")[0] for c in comps)))
    print("scope", dict(collections.Counter(c.get("scope", "required") for c in comps)))
    print("origin", dict(collections.Counter(p["value"] for c in comps
          for p in c.get("properties", []) if p["name"] == "chaos:origin")))
    print("source-kind", dict(collections.Counter(p["value"] for c in comps
          for p in c.get("properties", []) if p["name"] == "chaos:cargo:source-kind")))
    edges = sum(len(x["dependsOn"]) for x in d["dependencies"])
    print("dependency objects", len(d["dependencies"]), "edges", edges)
    print("root dependsOn", len(d["dependencies"][0]["dependsOn"]))
    print("timestamp key present:", "timestamp" in d["metadata"])
    print("components with hashes", sum(1 for c in comps if c.get("hashes")))
    print("hash algorithms", dict(collections.Counter(h["alg"] for c in comps for h in c.get("hashes", []))))
    print("hashed by origin", dict(collections.Counter(
        p["value"] for c in comps for p in c["properties"]
        if p["name"] == "chaos:origin" and c.get("hashes"))))
    print("components with hashes whose source-kind is not registry",
          sum(1 for c in comps if c.get("hashes") and all(
              p["value"] != "registry" for p in c["properties"]
              if p["name"] == "chaos:cargo:source-kind")))
    PY
    components 1227
    purl namespaces {'pkg:cargo': 1130, 'pkg:generic': 97}
    scope {'required': 1211, 'optional': 16}
    origin {'dependency': 1134, 'vendored': 5, 'workspace': 88}
    source-kind {'registry': 1130, 'git': 4, 'path': 93}
    dependency objects 1228 edges 5130
    root dependsOn 29
    timestamp key present: False
    components with hashes 1130
    hash algorithms {'SHA-256': 1130}
    hashed by origin {'dependency': 1130}
    components with hashes whose source-kind is not registry 0

Five things in that reading are decisions rather than facts, and each is asserted by the
guard:

- 1227 components, not 1228. The root crate is reachable and is one of the 1228 package ids,
  but it is emitted as `metadata.component` (the product) and not as a component. Listing it
  twice would let a scanner count the product among its own dependencies and would force a
  second answer to "which of these is the thing we ship".
- `pkg:generic` for 97 of them: 4 git dependencies and 93 path dependencies, the 88 workspace
  members plus 5 vendored crates. A `pkg:cargo` purl is a claim that crates.io serves that
  name at that version, which is false for a crate we patched; a scanner told `pkg:cargo`
  would resolve a different artifact than the one in the binary.
- `scope: optional` on 16: the 15 third-party packages reachable only across `build` edges,
  plus our own `xai-proto-build`. The kinds counted for this are restricted to the shipped
  subgraph -- a whole-graph reading can inherit a `build` kind through a dev-only parent.
- 29 direct dependencies of the product node, which is `xai-grok-pager-bin`'s own non-dev edge
  count, and is the number that makes section 2's `dependency edges: 0` impossible to accept.
- `hashes` on exactly 1130 components, every one of them `SHA-256` and every one of them from
  crates.io, and on zero components whose source is a git or a path. That last number is the
  half that matters: a digest would be a claim to have verified bytes, and for a crate read
  out of a directory nobody recorded which bytes. Section 8 follows the 1130 out to the files.

== 2. The defect this batch found in its own tool: an inventory with no graph

The first version of `depends()` filtered the children it emitted against a set of purls,
while the child lists it walked hold cargo package ids. The two sets do not intersect, so
every edge was dropped, and the run still exited 0. The defect is replayable on demand -- the
line is restored, the real generator is run against this workspace, and the file is written
back from the bytes read at the start:

    $ python3 /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/replay_defect.py
    $ python3 scripts/gen-sbom.py --output /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/defect-replay.json
    gen-sbom: wrote /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/defect-replay.json; components: 1227 (third-party 1134, vendored 5, workspace 88); dependency edges: 0; SHA-256 on 1130; serial urn:uuid:98e3a643-2aff-58a0-82e3-79d74a53e432
    exit: 0
    cmp after restore: identical

Two facts in that transcript are the point. `components: 1227` next to `dependency edges: 0`
is not a sparse graph: a workspace whose product alone has 29 direct dependencies cannot have
zero edges, so a reader who knows one of the two numbers can falsify the other. And the exit
code is 0: nothing about the run looked like a failure.

What caught it was generating against the real workspace. What was green at the time was the
fixture suite, because its expected edge count had been read off the generator's own output.
That number now comes from a hand-computed two-package graph (2 edges over root -> middle ->
leaf plus root -> leaf), and `RealWorkspace` asserts a floor no wrong answer clears, so the
durable protection is a floor plus an independent re-derivation rather than a remembered
number.

== 3. The guard refuses the defective document

Same file, run through `check-sbom.py`, which re-derives the edge set from `cargo metadata`
and compares in both directions:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-sbom.py --sbom /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/defect-replay.json
    check-sbom: FAIL (1 problem(s))
      the dependency graph is not the build's: 5130 edge(s) the build has are absent [pkg:cargo/addr2line@0.25.1 -> pkg:cargo/gimli@0.32.3, pkg:cargo/aes@0.9.0 -> pkg:cargo/cipher@0.5.1, pkg:cargo/aes@0.9.0 -> pkg:cargo/cpubits@0.1.1, pkg:cargo/aes@0.9.0 -> pkg:cargo/cpufeatures@0.3.0, pkg:cargo/agent-client-protocol-schema@0.11.4 -> pkg:cargo/anyhow@1.0.104, pkg:cargo/agent-client-protocol-schema@0.11.4 -> pkg:cargo/derive_more@2.1.1, pkg:cargo/agent-client-protocol-schema@0.11.4 -> pkg:cargo/schemars@1.0.4, pkg:cargo/agent-client-protocol-schema@0.11.4 -> pkg:cargo/serde@1.0.228 (and 5122 more)], 0 edge(s) the build does not have are present []
    exit: 1

The missing direction is reported with named examples and a count; the extra direction is
empty, which is the shape an accidental edge-drop should have. Cutting one edge out of a
correct document and adding one that the build does not have are separate fixtures
(`test_an_edge_the_build_has_must_be_in_the_document`,
`test_an_edge_the_build_does_not_have_must_not_be`), so a guard that only ever looked one way
would fail one of them.

== 4. Determinism: no timestamp by default, and a serial that moves with the content

The release workflow generates the SBOM in a different job from the one that checks it, so two
runs of one tree must produce one byte sequence. `metadata.timestamp` is therefore absent
unless asked for, and `serialNumber` is `urn:uuid:` plus a uuid5 over the sha256 of the
document with its own serial removed, keys sorted, separators tight:

    $ SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=1791158400 python3 scripts/gen-sbom.py --output /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/stamped.json
    gen-sbom: wrote /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/stamped.json; components: 1227 (third-party 1134, vendored 5, workspace 88); dependency edges: 5130; SHA-256 on 1130; serial urn:uuid:d9031ccf-d8a5-5355-9998-4ce99d3f4fa6
    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    import json
    d = json.load(open("/tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/stamped.json"))
    print("timestamp:", d["metadata"].get("timestamp"))
    print("serial:", d["serialNumber"])
    plain = json.load(open("/tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-a.json"))
    print("plain has timestamp key:", "timestamp" in plain["metadata"], "serial:", plain["serialNumber"])
    print("hashes in the stamped document:", sum(1 for c in d["components"] if c.get("hashes")))
    PY
    timestamp: 2026-10-05T00:00:00Z
    serial: urn:uuid:d9031ccf-d8a5-5355-9998-4ce99d3f4fa6
    plain has timestamp key: False serial: urn:uuid:1fc225f9-2cf6-5b21-89a7-231d3c4d50f4
    hashes in the stamped document: 1130

Stamping moves the serial, because the stamp is part of the content. That is the wanted
direction: the serial identifies this document, not this product, and section 2's replay shows
the serial changing when a dependency edge disappears. `--timestamp` is what
`.github/workflows/release.yml` uses, with the commit's committer date, so a release artifact
carries a date that is true about the commit rather than about the runner's clock.

== 5. The upstream schema, validated once, and deliberately not vendored

`check-sbom.py` is a hand-written structural and semantic validator. Before writing judgements
into it, the document was validated against the schema CycloneDX itself publishes, fetched to
a scratch directory and checked with the host's `jsonschema`:

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    import json, jsonschema
    schema = json.load(open("/tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b17/bom-1.6.schema.json"))
    doc = json.load(open("/tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-a.json"))
    v = jsonschema.Draft7Validator(schema)
    errs = list(v.iter_errors(doc))
    print("jsonschema", jsonschema.__version__, "errors:", len(errs))
    PY
    jsonschema 3.2.0 errors: 0

The schema file is 262 666 bytes and is not added to the repository. Two reasons, both about
obligations rather than convenience: a vendored schema is itself a redistributed third-party
artifact and would need its own entry in the notices document this batch just made exact, and
`jsonschema` is not installed in the CI container, so a schema-based check could not run there
anyway. The consequence is stated plainly: the online check borrows no external authority, so
its non-vacuity rests on sections 9 and 10 alone. Re-running section 5 needs the upstream file
and `pip install jsonschema`; the command above is here to be re-run, not believed.

== 6. What the guard imports, and why that is a claim

A generator cannot witness its own output, so the guard shares no code with it beyond
`scripts/notices_lib.py`, which is also what `check-notices-coverage.py` reads:

    $ grep -n '^import\|^from' scripts/ci/check-sbom.py
    54:from __future__ import annotations
    56:import argparse
    57:import hashlib
    58:import importlib.util
    59:import json
    60:import os
    61:import re
    62:import sys
    63:import uuid
    64:from pathlib import Path

`importlib.util` loads `notices_lib.py` by path; nothing loads `gen-sbom.py`. The guard
recomputes the package set, the edge kinds, the purls, the serial and the license expressions
from `cargo metadata`, then compares. Where the generator and the guard disagree, at least one
of them is wrong, and the disagreement is the finding.

== 7. The fixtures

`scripts/ci/test-check-sbom.py` builds a synthetic `cargo metadata` document, runs the real
generator over it, mutates the generated document, and runs the real guard on the result:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-sbom.py 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 70 tests in 7.027s

    OK

Ten classes, 70 cases. `Baseline` (3) pins the verdict string and the product identity;
`Coverage` (8) that a dev-only crate is absent and stays absent, that two versions of one name
are two components, that a git dependency is not claimed to come from crates.io;
`Truthfulness` (10) that license, `scope`, origin, duplicates and ref-shape lies are each
reported; `Digests` (17) that the published `hashes` are the lock's digests and only the
lock's, for the packages the lock has digests for; `Graph` (6) the product hanging off the
root crate plus a cut edge and an added edge; `Envelope` (5) the format, version and schema
pointer; `SerialNumber` (3) re-derivation and movement; `Determinism` (6) two byte-identical
runs, order independence, `--check`, and the timestamp rules; `FailClosed` (9) that a missing
file, a non-file, unreadable JSON and a bad root are failures rather than skips; and
`RealWorkspace` (3), which generates and checks against this repository, asserts the component
and third-party counts are both above 1000, runs both lock parsers over the shipped
`Cargo.lock` and requires them to agree, and hashes every `.crate` file in the local registry
cache against the digest the document publishes for it.

== 8. The digests: where they come from, and out to the bytes they name

`cargo metadata` publishes no checksum, so the `hashes` field cannot come from the build
description at all. It comes from `Cargo.lock`, which is where cargo records the digest of the
`.crate` it resolved for each registry package. The rule "only crates.io packages carry a
digest" is therefore a claim about that file, and the file gets measured before anything is
asserted:

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    import re, pathlib
    text = pathlib.Path("Cargo.lock").read_text()
    blocks = text.split("[[package]]")[1:]
    registry = reg_without = other = other_with = 0
    for block in blocks:
        source = re.search(r'^source = "([^"]*)"$', block, re.MULTILINE)
        digest = re.search(r'^checksum = "([0-9a-f]{64})"$', block, re.MULTILINE)
        if source and source.group(1).startswith("registry+"):
            registry += 1
            reg_without += 0 if digest else 1
        else:
            other += 1
            other_with += 1 if digest else 0
    print("package blocks", len(blocks))
    print("registry blocks", registry, "of which without a checksum", reg_without)
    print("non-registry blocks", other, "of which carrying a checksum", other_with)
    PY
    registry blocks 1225 of which without a checksum 0
    non-registry blocks 106 of which carrying a checksum 0

1225 registry entries and none of them is missing a digest; 106 non-registry entries and none
of them carries one. The generator turns either anomaly into a refusal rather than a judgement
call, so the 1130 published digests are the registry subset of those 1225 that the shipped
graph reaches (95 registry packages are in the lock and not in the binary), and no path or git
package picked a digest up on the way.

The two parsers are deliberately different programs -- `gen-sbom.py` splits the file on the
`[[package]]` marker and searches inside each block, `check-sbom.py` walks it line by line and
closes a record at the next table header -- because one shared parse would be one shared blind
spot, and the whole value of the digest is that two readings have to agree:

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    import importlib.util, pathlib, sys
    def load(name, path):
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(name, path)
        mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
        sys.modules[name] = mod
        spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
        return mod
    gen = load("gen_sbom", "scripts/gen-sbom.py")
    check = load("check_sbom", "scripts/ci/check-sbom.py")
    lock = pathlib.Path("Cargo.lock")
    a = gen.lock_digests(lock)
    b, problems = check.lock_digests(lock)
    print("gen-sbom digests", len(a))
    print("check-sbom digests", len(b), "problems", problems)
    print("identical", a == b)
    PY
    check-sbom digests 1225 problems []
    identical True

The guard also treats the lock as evidence rather than as gospel: a digest on a package that
came from no registry, one package listed twice with two digests, or a digest that is not 64
lowercase hex digits is each a finding, because in all three cases cargo did not write this
file. Those branches are fixtures
(`test_the_guard_refuses_a_lock_that_digests_a_package_cargo_has_no_digest_for`,
`test_the_guard_refuses_a_lock_that_lists_one_package_with_two_digests`,
`test_the_guard_refuses_a_lock_digest_that_is_not_hex`), and each of the three rules has its
own row in the section 9 matrix.

The last measurement in this section leaves the repository. Every digest the document
publishes is compared against the sha256 of the `.crate` file this host actually downloaded:

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    import hashlib, json, pathlib
    doc = json.load(open("/tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/final-a.json"))
    cache = pathlib.Path.home() / ".cargo" / "registry" / "cache"
    dirs = [d for d in sorted(cache.iterdir()) if d.is_dir()]
    same = missing = different = 0
    for c in doc["components"]:
        if not c.get("hashes"):
            continue
        name, version = c["name"], c["version"]
        crate = next((d / f"{name}-{version}.crate" for d in dirs
                      if (d / f"{name}-{version}.crate").is_file()), None)
        if crate is None:
            missing += 1
            continue
        if hashlib.sha256(crate.read_bytes()).hexdigest() == c["hashes"][0]["content"]:
            same += 1
        else:
            different += 1
    print("registry cache dirs", [d.name for d in dirs])
    print("crates on disk compared", same + different + missing)
    print("digests equal", same, "different", different, "not on disk", missing)
    PY
    crates on disk compared 1130
    digests equal 1130 different 0 not on disk 0

The same comparison runs inside the fixture suite as
`test_the_published_digests_match_the_downloaded_crates`, so it is not a claim about one
machine, except in the one sense that it needs the crates to have been fetched -- which is why
it lives in `RealWorkspace` rather than among the portable cases.

What the digest establishes, and what it does not, is worth stating exactly. It says: the
bytes the registry served for this name at this version hash to this value, and the same is
true of the copy sitting in the cache the build was resolved against. It does not say the
registry was honest. The lock's digest is the registry's own publication, so an index that
served tampered bytes together with a matching digest would produce a document exactly as
clean as this one. Checking against a second independent copy of the registry, or against
signatures, is outside what two off-line scripts can do, so neither of them claims it: what
they claim is agreement between the lock, the document, and the bytes on disk.

== 9. Mutation matrix against the guard: 24 planted weakenings, 0 survived

Each row weakens exactly one claim in `scripts/ci/check-sbom.py` and re-runs the 70 fixtures;
the file is restored from the bytes read at the start and verified with `cmp` after every row.
A row that no fixture noticed would mean an untested claim, so the honest fix would be a
fixture.

    $ python3 /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b18/mut.py
    ====================================================================================================
    coverage: never report a package the build reaches but the SBOM omits
        killed by: test_a_dropped_component_is_reported_by_name, test_the_example_list_is_capped_and_says_so
    origin: accept any chaos:origin the document claims
        killed by: test_a_wrong_origin_is_reported, test_the_origin_and_the_notices_membership_are_two_assertions
    third-party set: derive it from the build instead of from the document
        killed by: test_the_origin_and_the_notices_membership_are_two_assertions
    scope: treat every package as correctly scoped
        killed by: test_a_small_workspace_passes, test_the_product_is_named_from_the_manifest_it_ships, test_a_dev_only_dependency_is_absent_and_must_stay_absent, test_a_git_dependency_is_not_claimed_to_come_from_crates_io, test_a_vendored_crate_is_third_party_and_says_so, test_this_workspaces_own_crates_are_not_third_party, test_two_versions_of_one_name_are_two_components, test_a_timestamp_is_written_only_when_asked_for, test_the_document_is_ordered_by_origin_then_name_then_version, test_the_document_order_does_not_depend_on_the_input_order, test_registry_components_carry_the_digest_the_lock_records, test_a_document_on_stdin_is_read, test_the_shipped_binarys_sbom_describes_this_workspace, test_the_serial_re_derives_from_the_document, test_a_build_script_only_dependency_is_scope_optional, test_the_slash_shorthand_survives_as_an_spdx_expression
    license: stop comparing the expression with the package
        killed by: test_a_license_that_is_not_the_packages_is_reported
    purl namespace: stop checking the namespace against the source
        killed by: test_a_git_dependency_is_not_claimed_to_come_from_crates_io, test_a_registry_package_moved_to_the_other_namespace_is_reported
    purl shape: accept a ref that does not name this package
        killed by: test_a_purl_naming_some_other_package_is_reported
    bom-ref/purl agreement: accept the two naming different things
        killed by: test_a_bom_ref_that_is_not_the_purl_is_reported
    serial: stop re-deriving it from the document bytes
        killed by: test_any_content_change_moves_the_serial, test_the_serial_re_derives_from_the_document
    graph: compare the edges against nothing, so any graph passes
        killed by: test_an_edge_the_build_does_not_have_must_not_be, test_an_edge_the_build_has_must_be_in_the_document
    graph: accept a component with no dependency object
        killed by: test_every_component_needs_exactly_one_dependency_object
    product: accept a manifest name that is not the product's
        killed by: test_the_product_is_named_from_the_manifest_it_ships
    envelope: accept any specVersion
        killed by: test_another_spec_version_is_refused
    membership: accept a component for a package the build does not reach
        killed by: test_a_dev_only_dependency_is_absent_and_must_stay_absent
    membership: accept the root crate listed as a component
        killed by: test_the_binarys_own_crate_is_the_product_not_a_component
    duplicates: accept the same package listed twice
